% introduction.tex — expanded for 8-page target

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn jailbreak attacks pose a qualitatively different threat to large language model (LLM) safety than their single-turn counterparts. Attacks such as Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, Foot-in-the-Door (FITD)~\citep{fitd2024}, and Deceptive Delight~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns through rapport building, logical reframing, and incremental escalation. Persuasion is the common mechanism: \citet{zeng2024pap} catalogue 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} find identifiable strategies in 97\% of successful attacks. Individual attack turns occupy the same embedding region as benign text~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}, rendering per-turn safety filters fundamentally blind.

Production classifiers such as Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, and ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma} operate on individual turns and cannot model manipulation trajectories across exchanges. Recent multi-turn defenses have begun to address conversation-level structure: DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026} combines fine-tuned BERT with a multi-layer GRU, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid} infers turn-level intentions, and G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025} applies graph neural networks. These systems achieve reasonable in-distribution performance but are evaluated on single attack families and do not assess cross-dataset OOD transfer. None systematically analyze \emph{why} some representations generalize while others fail. The open question is: \emph{what representation properties enable cross-attack generalization?}

A vanilla DeBERTa encoder~\citep{he2021deberta} with temporal aggregation, representing the class of pretrained-encoder-plus-sequence-model approaches without domain adaptation (inspired by but not directly replicating DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026}, which differs in encoder fine-tuning and loss function), collapses from F1\,=\,1.000 on in-distribution data to F1\,=\,0.312$\pm$0.026 on out-of-distribution (OOD) Deceptive Delight, worsening with more turns (0.628 at turn~1 to 0.312 at full conversation). LLM-as-judge approaches fare no better: GPT-5.5 achieves F1 of only 0.191--0.449 on in-distribution detection with 30--47\% unparseable responses (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}). Content-level reasoning is insufficient for this task.

% experiments.tex — D024 compressed for 8-page limit

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Data.}
All synthetic data is generated by Qwen3-8B~\citep{qwen3_2025}.
The IID dataset contains 250 Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo} jailbreak and 250 topic-anchored benign conversations, split into training (350), validation (74), and test (76) sets with balanced class ratios (e.g., test = 38 jailbreak + 38 benign).
Stage~1 (DAPT) uses training + validation conversations; Stage~2 (BiGRU) trains on training, early-stops on validation, and evaluates on test.
OOD evaluation pairs attack-specific jailbreak conversations with the 38 IID-test benign conversations: 80 FITD~\citep{fitd2024} + 38 benign = 118 total, 80 Deceptive Delight (DD)~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} + 38 benign = 118 total, and 80 ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} + 38 benign = 118 total.
No overlap exists in attack templates across families.
The OOD benign set is IID (same generation pipeline as training); the OOD challenge is in distinguishing novel attack families from familiar benign conversations.
A paraphrased version of the IID test set (BERTScore 0.92, lexical overlap 0.20) serves as the adversarial robustness benchmark (Section~\ref{sec:perturbation}).
For human data validation, we use ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (204 jailbreak + 204 benign from real LMSYS user interactions), entirely independent of Qwen3-8B generation.

\paragraph{Models.}
Seven DeBERTa-v3 variants share the same Stage-2 pipeline and differ only in Stage-1 training (Section~\ref{sec:method}).
For cross-encoder validation, we replicate vanilla, MLM, and 9-class variants on RoBERTa-base~\citep{liu2019roberta} (Section~\ref{sec:cross_encoder}).
We also compare TF-IDF LR and LLM-as-judge baselines (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}): GPT-5.5 achieves only F1 = 0.191--0.449 on IID Crescendo with 30--47\% unparseable responses; on OOD data, zero-shot Qwen3-8B achieves macro F1 = 0.244 (DD and ActorAttack) by predicting nearly all conversations as benign, and even 3-shot prompting reaches only 0.566 (DD) and 0.652 (ActorAttack). Content-level reasoning is insufficient for this task.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
F1 for binary jailbreak detection at prefix $K$=2 and full conversations (full per-$K$ results in Appendix~\ref{sec:full_prefix}).
OOD results report 3-seed mean$\pm$std (seeds 42, 123, 456) for all variants.
For key pairwise comparisons, we report BCa bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals (10,000 resamples over individual predictions pooled across seeds) and paired permutation tests (10,000 permutations). Bootstrap means may differ slightly from seed-averaged F1 due to the nonlinearity of F1 aggregation.
All standard deviations are computed over 3 random seeds using the population formula ($n$ divisor); Bessel-corrected estimates ($n{-}1$ divisor) are $\approx$22\% larger but do not alter any significance conclusions.
Stage-1: learning rate $2 \times 10^{-5}$, batch 16, 4 epochs, MLM masking 15\%.
Stage-2: two-layer MLP, 20 epochs.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Cross-Attack Generalization}
\label{sec:cross_attack}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-attack generalization (F1) at $K$=2 and full conversation. 3-seed mean$\pm$std where noted. Full per-$K$ results in Appendix~\ref{sec:full_prefix}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc|cc@{}}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c|}{\textit{IID (Cresc.)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textit{OOD (DD)}} \\
\textbf{Method} & $K$=2 & Full & $K$=2 & Full \\
\midrule
9-class & \textbf{1.000$\pm$.000} & \textbf{1.000$\pm$.000} & .981$\pm$.013 & \textbf{.997$\pm$.005} \\
MLM-only$^{\ddagger}$ & .974 & 1.000 & \textbf{.993$\pm$.009} & .914$\pm$.115 \\
Vanilla & .978$\pm$.016 & 1.000$\pm$.000 & .479$\pm$.038 & .312$\pm$.026 \\
TF-IDF LR$^{\S}$ & 1.000 & 1.000 & 1.000 & .961 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{-2pt}
{\footnotesize $^{\ddagger}$MLM-only IID reports single seed (42); OOD reports 3-seed. $^{\S}$TF-IDF is deterministic (no std).}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} presents the core result.
All methods achieve near-perfect IID F1, reflecting inherent lexical separability when test matches training.
On OOD DD, vanilla DeBERTa collapses to F1 = 0.312$\pm$0.026, worsening with more turns (0.628$\pm$0.038 at $K$=1 to 0.312$\pm$0.026 at Full), consistent with the finding that attack turns occupy ``benign'' embedding regions~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}.
MLM achieves mean F1 $\geq$ 0.97 at all prefixes $K \leq 5$ without any classification labels; domain exposure alone is sufficient.
At full conversation length, MLM drops to 0.914$\pm$0.115 (seed 456 recall = 0.64), while the 9-class model maintains 0.997$\pm$0.005.
TF-IDF LR reaches F1 = 0.961 on full DD with near-perfect per-prefix performance ($K$=1--5: 0.994--1.000), confirming that DD is fully solvable by lexical features alone.
On structurally complex ActorAttack, TF-IDF collapses to F1 = 0.261 (precision = 1.0, recall = 0.15), while 9-class maintains 0.972$\pm$0.026: a 71\,pp gap. Lexical methods cannot capture multi-persona role-playing attack structure. This establishes a ``difficulty threshold'' for lexical methods parallel to that of vanilla encoders: both succeed on DD and fail on ActorAttack (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).
Bootstrap CIs confirm these gaps: 9-class DD Full 95\% CI [0.943, 1.000] vs.\ vanilla [0.202, 0.341]; 9-class ActorAttack Full CI [0.856, 1.000] vs.\ vanilla [0.203, 0.314]. Paired permutation tests yield $p < 0.0001$ for all prefix lengths on both datasets.
FITD is easier: 9-class and vanilla both reach F1 $\geq$ 0.990 at all prefixes.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_cross_attack_bar.pdf}
\caption{OOD Deceptive Delight F1 across prefix lengths for three representative variants. Vanilla DeBERTa degrades monotonically as more (benign-appearing) attack turns accumulate. MLM maintains high F1 at early prefixes but becomes unstable at full length. The 9-class model remains stable across all prefixes.}
\label{fig:cross_attack_bar}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:cross_attack_bar} visualizes the prefix-length trajectory on OOD DD.
The vanilla model's monotonic degradation from $K$=1 (0.628$\pm$0.038) to full conversation (0.312$\pm$0.026) confirms that additional attack turns, each individually benign-appearing, progressively confuse a non-adapted encoder.
MLM's strong early performance ($K$=2: 0.993) followed by instability at full length (0.914$\pm$0.115) suggests that domain exposure enables early detection of persuasion markers, but longer conversations introduce distributional drift that unsupervised pretraining cannot fully accommodate.
The 9-class model's flat trajectory (0.972--0.997 across all prefixes) demonstrates that classification supervision stabilizes representations against this drift.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Decomposing the Detection Mechanism}
\label{sec:decomposition}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Evidence hierarchy on OOD DD and ActorAttack (AA) at full conversation length (3-seed mean$\pm$std where available). All variants share the same Stage-2 pipeline. Full per-prefix results in Appendix Tables~\ref{tab:full_prefix} and~\ref{tab:actorattack_full}.}
\label{tab:evidence_hierarchy}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Variant} & DD-Full & AA-Full & DD-$K$=2 \\
\midrule
9-class (full model) & .997$\pm$.005 & .972$\pm$.026 & .981$\pm$.013 \\
Binary (2-class) & 1.000$\pm$.000 & .990$\pm$.008 & \textbf{.994$\pm$.009} \\
Scrambled labels & .997$\pm$.005 & .951$\pm$.043 & .981$\pm$.026 \\
MLM-only (no labels) & .914$\pm$.115 & .893$\pm$.080 & .993$\pm$.009 \\
Mean pooling$^{*}$ & .991$\pm$.013 & .962$\pm$.015 & 1.000 \\
\midrule
Topic classif.\ (5-class) & .541$\pm$.207 & .625$\pm$.141 & .593$\pm$.166 \\
Wikipedia MLM & .904$\pm$.115 & .686$\pm$.297 & .911$\pm$.073 \\
Vanilla DeBERTa & .312$\pm$.026 & .258$\pm$.000 & .479$\pm$.038 \\
TF-IDF LR$^{\dagger}$ & .961 & .261 & 1.000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{-2pt}
{\footnotesize $^{*}$9-class encoder with mean pooling replacing BiGRU. $^{\dagger}$Deterministic (no std).}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:evidence_hierarchy} reports F1 for all variants on DD and ActorAttack. The \emph{evidence hierarchy} (ordering of variants by OOD detection performance) at full conversation length on DD is:

\vspace{2pt}
\noindent\hspace{0.5em}{\small vanilla (0.31$\pm$0.03) $<$ topic (0.54$\pm$0.21) $\ll$ wiki-MLM (0.90$\pm$0.12) $\approx$ jb-MLM (0.91$\pm$0.12) $<$ scrambled (0.997$\pm$0.005) $=$ 9-class (0.997$\pm$0.005) $\approx$ binary (1.000$\pm$0.000)}
\vspace{2pt}

\paragraph{Domain exposure is the primary mechanism.}
MLM bridges $\sim$60\,pp from vanilla (0.312$\pm$0.026 $\to$ 0.914$\pm$0.115) without labels, confirming the DAPT interpretation~\citep{gururangan2020dontstop}. TF-IDF collapse on ActorAttack (0.261 vs.\ 9-class 0.972) confirms that domain-adapted representations capture features beyond lexical overlap.
All three classification-supervised variants converge to equivalent detection (F1 = 0.997--1.000); McNemar's test finds no significant pairwise differences (all $p > 0.13$; $\leq$7 discordant pairs limit power), and paired permutation tests confirm equivalence ($p \geq 0.40$). TOST equivalence tests (margin = 5\,pp) confirm statistical equivalence on DD (all $p < 0.01$, 90\% CI within $\pm$0.05). On ActorAttack, equivalence cannot be statistically confirmed due to limited seeds ($n$=3), though observed mean differences (2--4\,pp) fall within practical equivalence margins. Label quality and granularity are non-factors for detection.
Classification supervision reduces cross-seed variance 4--20$\times$ over MLM on synthetic OOD (std 0.005 vs.\ 0.115 on DD; bootstrap CI widths: 5.7\,pp vs.\ 35.5\,pp). This stability benefit does not transfer to human-authored data, where classification-supervised variants exhibit the highest variance and pure MLM the lowest (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}).
Appendix~\ref{app:tsne} provides t-SNE visualizations confirming that domain-adapted variants produce tighter, more separable embedding clusters than the vanilla baseline.

\paragraph{Domain specificity is marginal at full length but meaningful for early detection.}
Wikipedia MLM (0.904$\pm$0.115) nearly matches jailbreak-domain MLM at full length (1\,pp gap); general continued pretraining provides most of the representational benefit.
At early prefixes, jailbreak-domain text provides an 8\,pp advantage ($K$=2: 0.993 vs.\ 0.911), consistent with the interpretation that domain-specific features (persuasion markers, role-play framing) are most informative when the model has seen fewer turns.
As conversations grow longer, generic linguistic features accumulated through any continued pretraining become sufficient for discrimination.
Perturbation analysis (Section~\ref{sec:perturbation}) reveals a sharper domain specificity effect: Wikipedia MLM degrades by $\sim$12\,pp under adversarial paraphrasing while jailbreak-domain MLM degrades by only 2.6\,pp.

\paragraph{Misaligned supervision destabilizes generalization.}
Topic classification yields DD-Full F1 = 0.541$\pm$0.207; seed 123 falls \emph{below} vanilla (0.252 vs.\ 0.312). Despite using the same jailbreak-domain training data, topic classification performs worse than Wikipedia MLM (0.904$\pm$0.115), which uses out-of-domain text with no labels. A misaligned objective forces the encoder to allocate capacity to irrelevant features and actively destabilizes representations that MLM would preserve.

\paragraph{Evidence hierarchy on structurally complex attacks.}
ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} uses multi-persona steering across longer conversation structures, providing a more challenging OOD benchmark than DD.
The evidence hierarchy at full conversation length is:

\vspace{2pt}
\noindent\hspace{0.5em}{\small TF-IDF (0.26) $\approx$ vanilla (0.26$\pm$0.00) $<$ topic (0.63$\pm$0.14) $<$ wiki-MLM (0.69$\pm$0.30) $<$ jb-MLM (0.89$\pm$0.08) $<$ scrambled (0.95$\pm$0.04) $\approx$ 9-class (0.97$\pm$0.03) $\approx$ binary (0.99$\pm$0.01)}
\vspace{2pt}

TF-IDF collapses from 0.961 (DD) to 0.261 (ActorAttack, precision = 1.0, recall = 0.15), confirming that domain-adapted representations capture features beyond bag-of-words.
All classification-supervised variants converge to equivalent performance (0.951--0.990, max gap 3.9\,pp), extending the DD finding. DAPT provides $\sim$71\,pp over both vanilla (0.258) and TF-IDF (0.261). Full per-prefix results in Appendix~\ref{sec:actorattack_full}.

\paragraph{Temporal modeling.}
Three-seed evaluation on both DD and ActorAttack confirms that mean pooling matches BiGRU (F1 gap $\leq$ 1\,pp on full conversations, overlapping CIs), indicating that detection relies on per-turn representations rather than their sequential order.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Cross-Encoder Validation}
\label{sec:cross_encoder}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-encoder comparison on OOD Full (3-seed mean$\pm$std). DD = Deceptive Delight, AA = ActorAttack. RoBERTa vanilla collapses on structurally complex ActorAttack despite strong DD performance.}
\label{tab:cross_encoder}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc|cc@{}}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c|}{DeBERTa-v3} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{RoBERTa} \\
\textbf{Variant} & DD & AA & DD & AA \\
\midrule
Vanilla & .312$\pm$.026 & .258$\pm$.000 & .981$\pm$.026 & .466$\pm$.163 \\
MLM & .914$\pm$.115 & .893$\pm$.080 & .987$\pm$.018 & .730$\pm$.201 \\
9-class & .997$\pm$.005 & .972$\pm$.026 & .994$\pm$.005 & .939$\pm$.046 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We replicate vanilla, MLM, and 9-class on RoBERTa-base~\citep{liu2019roberta} (Table~\ref{tab:cross_encoder}).
On DD, RoBERTa vanilla already achieves 0.981$\pm$0.026 (vs.\ DeBERTa 0.312$\pm$0.026), so DAPT adds only 1.3\,pp. ActorAttack reveals this as DD's simplicity: RoBERTa vanilla collapses to 0.466$\pm$0.163, a 51.5\,pp drop, while domain-adapted variants recover (9-class: 0.939$\pm$0.046).

Encoder architecture (and associated capacity differences of 184M vs.\ 125M parameters) determines the \emph{difficulty threshold} at which domain adaptation becomes critical: DeBERTa's RTD pretraining~\citep{he2023debertav3,clark2020electra} requires adaptation even for simple attacks, while RoBERTa's MLM pretraining~\citep{liu2019roberta} tolerates simple attacks but fails on structurally complex ones. Bootstrap CIs confirm: RoBERTa vanilla DD 95\% CI [0.883, 1.000] vs.\ DeBERTa vanilla [0.202, 0.341], no overlap.
Classification supervision provides a consistent stability benefit across both encoders (cross-seed variance: 0.005--0.046 vs.\ 0.026--0.201).

Centered CKA~\citep{kornblith2019cka} confirms that both DAPT and classification supervision substantially reshape representations, yet comparable CKA shifts yield different detection gains depending on the starting point (Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_heatmap}).
Linear probing on diagnostic tasks shows 9-class outperforms vanilla and MLM by 1--5\,pp; MLM reshapes representations without improving diagnostic separability.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Human Data Validation}
\label{sec:human_data}

Since both training and OOD evaluation data are Qwen3-8B-generated, we validate on ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (204 human-written jailbreak attempts + 204 benign conversations from real LMSYS interactions).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Human data validation on ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (macro F1). 3-seed mean$\pm$std. Single-turn data from real LMSYS user interactions, independent of Qwen3-8B.}
\label{tab:human_data}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Variant} & Macro F1 \\
\midrule
JB-MLM & .837$\pm$.013 \\
Wiki-MLM & .791$\pm$.093 \\
Binary & .705$\pm$.072 \\
Scrambled & .616$\pm$.056 \\
9-class & .596$\pm$.087 \\
Topic & .496$\pm$.042 \\
\midrule
Vanilla & .413$\pm$.021 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:human_data} reports macro F1 on ToxicChat. Domain-adapted variants range from 0.596 to 0.837, while vanilla achieves 0.413. All DAPT variants exceed vanilla by 18--42\,pp, refuting the hypothesis that the evidence hierarchy is a Qwen3-8B generation artifact: DAPT on synthetic jailbreak text transfers to human-written attacks.

The relative ordering among domain-adapted variants differs from multi-turn evaluations: JB-MLM leads while classification-supervised variants rank lower.
The ranking reversal is statistically significant: JB-MLM outperforms 9-class by +24.1\,pp ($p < 0.001$, permutation test; 95\% CI [0.816, 0.858] vs.\ [0.569, 0.624], non-overlapping), and Wiki-MLM outperforms 9-class by +20.1\,pp ($p < 0.001$).
JB-MLM exhibits the lowest cross-seed variance (std = 0.013 vs.\ 0.087 for 9-class), confirming that unsupervised domain adaptation produces more stable representations on out-of-distribution human data.
ToxicChat contains single-turn direct attacks with no multi-turn escalation structure, so the classification head's shaping of turn-sequence representations provides no advantage. MLM captures token-level distributional properties that transfer to single-turn detection without relying on sequential patterns. This is a modality mismatch, not a finding about variant quality: the multi-turn pipeline's full evidence hierarchy requires multi-turn human data to validate.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Perturbation Analysis}
\label{sec:perturbation}

Under adversarial paraphrasing (BERTScore 0.92, lexical overlap 0.20), classification-supervised variants achieve zero F1 degradation at full conversation length on IID and OOD DD, while non-adapted models degrade by $\sim$12\,pp (McNemar 9-class vs.\ vanilla $p < 0.001$ on OOD DD).
On ActorAttack, absolute robustness does not hold (9-class $\Delta$\,=\,$-$20.4$\pm$13.5\,pp), but perturbation narrows rather than closes the adapted vs.\ non-adapted gap (9-class perturbed F1 = 0.768 vs.\ vanilla 0.248, $p < 0.001$).
Absolute perturbation robustness is attack-family-dependent; DAPT's detection advantage persists under paraphrasing.
Full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:perturbation_full}.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Strategy Trajectory Analysis}
\label{sec:strategy}

The 9-class persuasion taxonomy provides interpretable characterization of known attack families ($\kappa$=0.78, $n$=50; Section~\ref{sec:method}).
Transition count matrices reveal distinct orchestration patterns: Crescendo cycles broadly across all strategy categories, FITD concentrates on Role$\to$Obfuscation, and DD on Emotion$\to$Obfuscation (Appendix~\ref{sec:strategy_full}).
On unseen attacks, strategy prediction degrades to near-random ($\kappa$=0.126 on OOD DD), consistent with the detection mechanism analysis: all classification-supervised variants achieve equivalent detection F1 (0.997--1.000) regardless of strategy prediction quality. Detection operates through domain-adapted \texttt{[CLS]} embeddings, not through strategy classification accuracy.

